# Nanoindentation with a spherical indenter — reference implementation.
#
# Four details matter, and the last two are invisible without running it.
#
#   * A free surface in z (boundary p p f) is required: with periodic z there is
#     no surface and the indenter presses into bulk material.
#   * Atoms are created only in the substrate, leaving vacuum for the indenter.
#   * The bottom layer is ANCHORED. With a free z boundary both z faces are open,
#     so an unanchored slab starting at z = 0 bleeds atoms out of the bottom.
#     LAMMPS reports "Lost atoms: original 648 current 624" and the static
#     validator sees nothing wrong. This is Level 2 work, not Level 1.
#   * The indenter starts just above the surface, with a force constant suited to
#     reduced Lennard-Jones units. K = 1000 is enormous here.

units           lj
atom_style      atomic
boundary        p p f

lattice         fcc 0.85

# 6 x 6 x 12 lattice units = 10.06 x 10.06 x 20.11 LJ. The z headroom is the
# vacuum the indenter descends through.
region          box block 0 6 0 6 0 12
create_box      1 box

# Substrate: 0..4 lattice units in z = 0..6.70 LJ.
region          substrate block 0 6 0 6 0 4
create_atoms    1 region substrate
mass            1 1.0

pair_style      lj/cut 2.5
pair_coeff      1 1 1.0 1.0 2.5

# The lower lattice unit is held fixed, as in a real indentation experiment
# where the specimen rests on a rigid base. Everything above it is free.
region          base block 0 6 0 6 0 1
group           base region base
group           mobile subtract all base

velocity        mobile create 0.3 4928459 loop geom
velocity        base set 0.0 0.0 0.0
fix             hold base setforce 0.0 0.0 0.0

neighbor        0.3 bin
neigh_modify    delay 0 every 20 check no

# The indenter centre starts at z = 11.8 LJ, so with radius 5.0 its lowest point
# sits just above the substrate top at 6.70. It descends 0.0002 per step.
variable        zind equal 11.8-0.0002*step

# Thermostat the mobile substrate throughout: an unthermostatted substrate heats
# locally and softens, corrupting the force response being measured.
fix             1 mobile nvt temp 0.3 0.3 0.3
fix             2 mobile indent 10.0 sphere 5.027 5.027 v_zind 5.0

timestep        0.003
thermo          100
thermo_style    custom step temp pe etotal f_2[1] f_2[2] f_2[3]
run             3000
